<section class="gr-article-section" id="gretsch-model-specific-serials">
 <h2>Model Families And Serial Number Exceptions</h2>
 <p>Gretsch’s main systems cover many models. The practical differences are the number’s location, the production period and whether a particular run followed that system. Start with the family below, then compare the complete marking with the era charts.</p>
 <div class="gr-table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Gretsch model families and serial checks"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Model Family</th><th scope="col">Where To Start</th><th scope="col">Exception To Check</th></tr></thead><tbody>{families.map(row=><tr><th scope="row">{row[0]}</th><td>{row[1]}</td><td>{row[2]}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
 <p>The <a href="/gretsch-labels-and-serial-number-locations/">label and serial-location photographs</a> show the physical clues behind these checks. Gretsch’s <a href="https://blog.gretschguitars.com/2016/05/gretschtech-serial-numbers-1930s-1966/">early-number history</a> explains the overlapping chart and misplaced-label problem. For later model names, compare the <a href="/gretsch-vintage-vs-reissue/">original-versus-reissue guide</a>.</p>
 <h3 id="gretsch-modern-variants">Modern Prefixes Beyond The Older Factory Table</h3>
 <p>The official two-letter table is useful, but it does not include every marking seen on later instruments. The examples below are instrument records, not a new universal factory chart. Our lookup keeps their full prefixes and distinguishes a possible date reading from a standard documented result.</p>
 <div class="gr-table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Additional observed Gretsch prefixes"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Prefix</th><th scope="col">Evidence And Example</th><th scope="col">Lookup Treatment</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><td>UC</td><td><a href="https://reverb.com/item/91984140-pre-owned-2015-gretsch-custom-shop-g6128cs-1959-duo-jet-relic-champagne-sparkle-with-ohsc">Truetone’s U.S. Custom Shop Duo Jet</a>, UC15051228.</td><td>Possible May 2015; verify the certificate and full specification.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>IS</td><td>Indonesian prefix included in <a href="https://reverb.com/news/how-to-date-a-gretsch-guitar">Reverb’s dating reference</a>.</td><td>Possible year/month reading. The tool does not infer a current factory operator from this older reference.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>CYG</td><td><a href="https://musicgoround.com/locations/Winnipeg-mb/product/S000013315/Gretsch-ELECTROMATIC">Electromatic listed with CYG22020242</a>.</td><td>Retain all three letters. The old month formula is not verified for this prefix; check the factory record.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>ISG</td><td><a href="https://bluenotemusic.com/products/gretsch-g2210-streamliner-junior-jet-club-in-vintage-sunburst-no-case">G2210 Streamliner listed with ISG210723138</a>.</td><td>Keep every digit. Do not assume 07 is a month or that this run uses a four-digit sequence.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>IWA And Other Extended Markings</td><td><a href="https://reverb.com/item/88122701-new-gretsch-jim-dandy-concert-rex-burst-support-a-brick-and-mortar-music-store">Jim Dandy listing with IWA2503557WH</a>.</td><td>Manual confirmation. Keep the suffix, model and country information; no exact factory/date formula is asserted.</td></tr>
 </tbody></table></div>
 <p>A dealer listing can contain a transcription error, and one instrument does not prove a rule for every production run. In particular, the CYG and ISG examples establish the markings, not a month formula. Use the <a href="https://serialnumberlookup.fender.com/">manufacturer’s serial-record search</a> or contact Gretsch with the complete number. CMG, CYGC, IOG and other unfamiliar markings should be photographed intact and checked with Gretsch. A failed lookup is not proof that the instrument is counterfeit.</p>
 <h3 id="gretsch-number-limitations">What The Serial Cannot Establish</h3>
 <p>A serial alone does not confirm original pickups, finish, hardware or neck. It does not establish a sale date, shipping date, Custom Shop certificate number or edition size. A prototype-range sequence needs factory records, and replacement labels or parts can separate the number from the original instrument.</p>
 <p>For a useful follow-up, include front and back photographs, the complete serial and model label, headstock markings and any original paperwork. Photograph existing openings without removing glued parts. If internal access needs disassembly, have a guitar technician document it.</p>
 <h3 id="gretsch-tool-coverage">What This Lookup Covers</h3>
 <p>The tool compares overlapping vintage ranges, short date codes, Baldwin separators, later orange-label sequences, Japanese revival model groups and documented modern factory codes. It asks about the location and label when those details change the answer. UC and IS receive qualified readings; CYG and ISG need factory-date confirmation; unsupported import and acoustic formats receive a request for model evidence.</p>
